{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgS95bmX3hKeYL3PHoUDe","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The system prompt is now split into two cached blocks, so a `save_fact` should only re-cache the per-athlete tail, not the instructions. I haven't confirmed this against the live API, and the fix may not take effect if the static block is under the model's minimum cacheable length.\n\n**What changed:** In `bot.py`, `build_system()` now returns two text blocks, each with its own `cache_control`:\n1. `INSTRUCTIONS`: a constant, identical for every athlete and day.\n2. `athlete_context(...)`: the date, profile, zones, goal and facts. This is the only block a memory write changes.\n\nI checked this offline. The static block was byte-identical after a fact was added, for another athlete, and on another day. The tail changed after the fact was added. I did not touch `data/athletes.json` or `logs/usage.jsonl`, and I did not commit.\n\n**Open risk:** Anthropic won't cache a prefix shorter than the model's minimum (1024 tokens for Sonnet). The prefix includes the tool schemas. My rough estimate is that the instructions plus core tools land near 900 tokens, but I couldn't measure it without an API key. If it is under the minimum, the first marker caches nothing and `save_fact` still evicts everything. ANSWER.md explains how to check this from the first `usage.jsonl` row after one normal message.\n\n**Not changed:** The keyword tool filter still changes the tool list between messages, which busts the whole cache for that call. The log shows this on the `plan_week` call. `NOTES.md` says to keep the filter, so I left it.\n\n**Colleague's note:** I used the two-marker split and the rule against interpolated values in the static block. The Telegram and MCP details don't apply to this bot. Their token counts don't match this bot's sizes, and the same minimum-length caution applies to their approach. Details are in ANSWER